Pad SMSG_LFG_PROPOSAL_UPDATE to five members, and send the role bitmask the
3.3.5 client actually tests.
Touches: src/server/game/Handlers/LFGHandler.cpp, WorldSession::SendLfgUpdateProposal()

(The filename says "index" because the 0.1.74 attempt below believed the role
field was a table index. It is not. The name is kept so numbering stays stable.)

Why: players saw this on every dungeon-finder pop --

  Interface\FrameXML\LFGFrame.lua:337: Unknown role: UNKNOWN
  ... GetTexCoordsForRole -> LFDDungeonReadyStatus_UpdateIcon
      -> LFDDungeonReadyPopup_Update

THE ROOT CAUSE, in one sentence: the 3.3.5 client hard-codes the number of
proposal members at five, and Bonesaw's queue deliberately pops proposals with
fewer than five, so every unfilled slot is read out of client memory that no
packet ever wrote and comes back with no role.

HOW THAT WAS ESTABLISHED, after four wrong guesses that were all guesses.

FrameXML, extracted from the client's own MPQs -- which needs
MPQ_OPEN_READ_ONLY while the client is running, or the files merely look absent
(tools/client-patch/extract_framexml.py):

  * LFGFrame.lua:337 `GetTexCoordsForRole(role)` takes a STRING, accepts only
    GUIDE/TANK/HEALER/DAMAGER, and error()s on anything else.
  * Its only two dynamic callers are LFDFrame.lua:539 (role from
    `GetLFGProposal()`, i.e. your own) and LFDFrame.lua:678 in
    `LFDDungeonReadyStatus_UpdateIcon` (role from `GetLFGProposalMember(i)`).
  * LFDDungeonReadyPopup_Update drives the second one:

        for i=1, numMembers do LFDDungeonReadyStatus_UpdateIcon(...Player"..i)
        for i=numMembers+1, NUM_LFD_MEMBERS do ...Player"..i:Hide()

Then Wow.exe 3.3.5a 12340 was disassembled instead of being reasoned about
(tools/client-patch/dump_lfg_role_table.py):

  * `GetLFGProposalMember` (0x00554100-ish) bounds-checks its argument against
    a CONSTANT: `cmp eax, 5 / jae bail` at 0x0055306b. It does not know or care
    how many members the packet carried; slot 4 is as readable as slot 1.
  * `GetLFGProposal` does not return the packet's member count either. It
    pushes a constant -- `fld qword [0x009f5988]` at 0x005542fd, and that qword
    is 5.0. So `numMembers` is ALWAYS 5, the draw loop always runs five times,
    and the loop that would hide unused rows is 6..5 and never runs.
  * The role field is turned into a string by a BIT TEST, identical at both
    call sites (0x00553093 and 0x00554255):

        a8 02              test al, 2
        8b 0d b0 87 ad 00  mov  ecx, [0x00ad87b0]   ; "TANK"
        a8 04              test al, 4
        8b 15 b4 87 ad 00  mov  edx, [0x00ad87b4]   ; "HEALER"
        a8 08              test al, 8
        a1 b8 87 ad 00     mov  eax, [0x00ad87b8]   ; "DAMAGER"
        68 10 41 9e 00     push 0x009e4110          ; "UNKNOWN"

    Those bits are exactly AzerothCore's PLAYER_ROLE_*. Bit 0 is read
    separately (`and eax, 1`) as the isLeader return, so the leader bit belongs
    in the field. "UNKNOWN" is reachable only from a value with none of bits
    2/4/8 -- which is what a slot the packet never wrote contains.

Retail never hit this because a retail proposal is always exactly five members.
Bonesaw's is not: `LFGQueue::CheckCompatibility` computes
`allowIncomplete = hasSoloQueue() || raidQueue || allowBotFill` (LFGQueue.cpp:339)
and pops short, filling the group with bots on accept. The live log for one
uptime shows ten proposals, every one of them two members -- so every pop cost
the player three Lua errors, one per empty seat.

THE FIX. Always write MAXGROUPSIZE member blocks. Real members first, in the
existing tank/healer/dps order; the remainder are written as accepted dps,
which is what the bot fill is about to make them. Nothing is invented that the
group will not actually become, and the popup stops showing seats waiting
forever on players who do not exist yet.

The same change caps the list at MAXGROUPSIZE for raid queues, since the client
cannot address a sixth slot at all, and keeps the recipient inside the cap --
`GetLFGProposal` finds "you" by scanning those same five slots for the self
flag (0x00554220), and finds nothing useful if you were truncated away.

THE FOUR EARLIER ATTEMPTS, all aimed at the role VALUE, which was never wrong.

  0.1.72  Narrowed a multi-bit mask to one role bit. Fixed nothing: the mask was
          already a single clean bit, and the "corrected a mask" line this added
          never fired once across a full uptime.
  0.1.73  Also stripped PLAYER_ROLE_LEADER. Fixed nothing, and silently cost the
          ready dialog its leader crown, since bit 0 is the isLeader return.
  0.1.74  Sent 0/1/2 as a table index. This one CAUSED errors rather than fixing
          them: index 2 ("damage") is the TANK bit, so rogues drew tank icons,
          and indices 0 and 1 carry no role bit, so tanks and healers threw this
          very error. The rogue-as-tank sighting that got it reverted was the
          index theory refuting itself.
  0.1.75  Reverted to a clean 2/4/8 and instrumented every proposal at error
          level. The instrumentation is what finally closed this -- not by
          showing a bad value, but by showing that every value was GOOD and
          every proposal had two members.

The lesson is the standing one: four theories were built on what the server
sends without once reading what the client does with it. Ten minutes of
disassembly ended it. Both restored: the leader bit, and the mask untouched.

--- a/src/server/game/Handlers/LFGHandler.cpp
+++ b/src/server/game/Handlers/LFGHandler.cpp
@@ ProposalRoleForClient -- stop rewriting a value that was never wrong
-static uint8 ProposalRoleForClient(uint8 role)
-{
-    if (role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK)
-        return lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK;
-    if (role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER)
-        return lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER;
-    return lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_DAMAGE;
-}
+static uint8 ProposalRoleForClient(uint8 role)
+{
+    if (role & (lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_DAMAGE))
+        return role;
+
+    return uint8((role & lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_LEADER) | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_DAMAGE);
+}

@@ SendLfgUpdateProposal -- the count is a constant on the client, so send it
-    data << uint8(proposal.players.size());                // Group size
+    data << uint8(MAXGROUPSIZE);                           // Group size

@@ SendLfgUpdateProposal -- the client cannot address a sixth slot
+    if (ordered.size() > size_t(MAXGROUPSIZE))
+    {
+        auto const self = std::find(ordered.begin(), ordered.end(), guid);
+        if (self != ordered.end() && std::distance(ordered.begin(), self) >= MAXGROUPSIZE)
+            std::iter_swap(ordered.begin() + (MAXGROUPSIZE - 1), self);
+        ordered.resize(MAXGROUPSIZE);
+    }

@@ SendLfgUpdateProposal -- fill the seats the bot fill is about to occupy
+    for (size_t filled = ordered.size(); filled < size_t(MAXGROUPSIZE); ++filled)
+    {
+        data << uint32(lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_DAMAGE);           // Role
+        data << uint8(0) << uint8(0) << uint8(0);          // not self, not in dungeon, not same group
+        data << uint8(1) << uint8(1);                      // answered, accepted
+    }
     SendPacket(&data);

@@ SendLfgUpdateProposal -- temporary instrumentation removed
-        ... LFGPROBE line per member, at LOG_ERROR ...
-        ... LFGBYTES full-packet hex dump, at LOG_ERROR ...
+        if (!(player.role & (lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_TANK | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_HEALER | lfg::PLAYER_ROLE_DAMAGE)))
+            LOG_ERROR("lfg", "... has no LFG role at all (mask {}), sending damage", ...);
